WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.842 --> 00:00:04.638
A position is what someone says they want: the report by Friday.

2
00:00:05.631 --> 00:00:09.160
An interest is why they want it: a board meeting on Monday.

3
00:00:10.161 --> 00:00:14.526
This is principled negotiation, the Harvard method: positions dig in,

4
00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:16.361
interests open options.

5
00:00:17.374 --> 00:00:20.126
So ask why, and why not: what would that give you?

6
00:00:21.172 --> 00:00:25.038
Listen for needs such as security, control, money and time,

7
00:00:25.236 --> 00:00:26.977
and write them next to yours.

8
00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:32.137
Shared and different interests both help: one cares about the date,

9
00:00:32.311 --> 00:00:33.658
the other about cost.

10
00:00:34.787 --> 00:00:37.039
Then trade: give what is cheap for you.

11
00:00:37.725 --> 00:00:41.451
Put several issues on the table at once, in a package deal.

12
00:00:42.473 --> 00:00:46.037
Use a fair standard, like a market rate or past data,

13
00:00:46.153 --> 00:00:48.823
so both can say yes without losing face.

14
00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:55.121
Splitting the difference early feels fair, but rewards whoever opened most extreme.

15
00:00:56.103 --> 00:00:58.088
Chris Voss: emotions decide.

16
00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:02.493
Listen, name the feeling, and ask: what would this solve for you?

17
00:01:03.831 --> 00:01:08.162
At a grid operator you want every job checked two days ahead: permits,

18
00:01:08.254 --> 00:01:09.996
materials, switching plan.

19
00:01:10.988 --> 00:01:13.902
The head of execution says: my crews won't do it.

20
00:01:14.692 --> 00:01:16.724
His position: no extra day.

21
00:01:17.751 --> 00:01:19.585
Ask: what would that day cost you?

22
00:01:20.296 --> 00:01:23.210
He fears idle crews, and planners blaming them.

23
00:01:24.212 --> 00:01:26.592
Your interest: fewer wasted trips.

24
00:01:27.294 --> 00:01:30.952
So the planners check, and crews only get jobs that pass.

25
00:01:31.957 --> 00:01:34.766
Propose a pilot: four weeks, one region.

26
00:01:35.491 --> 00:01:38.441
Standard: wasted trips, before and after.

27
00:01:39.757 --> 00:01:43.484
Your BATNA is your best alternative to a negotiated agreement:

28
00:01:43.611 --> 00:01:45.991
what you actually do if you walk away.

29
00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:49.565
With a good alternative you say no calmly.

30
00:01:50.253 --> 00:01:52.227
A bad one, you improve first.

31
00:01:53.344 --> 00:01:57.547
From it follows your walk-away point: the worst deal you still accept.

32
00:01:58.616 --> 00:02:03.666
A client offers 950 euros a day; elsewhere you have a firm thousand.

33
00:02:04.727 --> 00:02:08.001
If nothing else differs, less than a thousand is worse.

34
00:02:08.724 --> 00:02:10.268
Decide before the meeting.

35
00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:15.820
Estimate theirs too: another supplier, doing it themselves, or waiting?

36
00:02:16.818 --> 00:02:21.265
Between both walk-away points lies the ZOPA: the zone of possible agreement.

37
00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.047
No ZOPA?

38
00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.683
Then no clever wording helps.

39
00:02:26.395 --> 00:02:27.823
Change what is on the table.

40
00:02:28.824 --> 00:02:29.614
Don't bluff.

41
00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:35.564
Mention a real alternative calmly, as information, and work on a second option.

42
00:02:36.591 --> 00:02:41.792
Switching cost, such as time, learning and qualification, weakens an alternative.

43
00:02:42.826 --> 00:02:45.229
A common failure: overrating your BATNA.

44
00:02:45.964 --> 00:02:49.146
Show a strong one calmly, keep a weak one to yourself.

45
00:02:50.486 --> 00:02:54.074
A manufacturer buys coated brackets from one supplier.

46
00:02:54.815 --> 00:02:56.522
It wants 12 percent more.

47
00:02:57.556 --> 00:03:02.003
A second quotes 4 percent above today, but needs 10 weeks to qualify.

48
00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:04.056
No coating in-house.

49
00:03:05.096 --> 00:03:06.350
Your BATNA: switching.

50
00:03:07.156 --> 00:03:08.909
Theirs: losing your volume.

51
00:03:09.934 --> 00:03:13.579
Start the qualification now: it improves your alternative.

52
00:03:14.615 --> 00:03:18.423
Ask what drives the 12 percent, and offer 4 percent now,

53
00:03:18.574 --> 00:03:20.873
with an index clause on the energy price.

54
00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:26.425
Your walk-away point is near the 4 percent, plus what qualifying costs.

55
00:03:27.712 --> 00:03:32.936
The first number is an anchor: it pulls every estimate along, even when arbitrary.

56
00:03:33.937 --> 00:03:36.213
The first offer strongly predicts the deal.

57
00:03:36.897 --> 00:03:39.997
If you know the market, name the first number yourself.

58
00:03:40.993 --> 00:03:43.059
Make it ambitious and defensible.

59
00:03:43.765 --> 00:03:47.817
Without a basis it costs credibility, sometimes the whole talk.

60
00:03:48.867 --> 00:03:51.549
A planning diagnostic at three offices.

61
00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.800
The last three took 18, 22 and 20 days.

62
00:03:56.844 --> 00:03:58.481
Purchasing said about 10.

63
00:03:59.219 --> 00:04:02.040
You: it took 18 to 22, so 20.

64
00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:06.733
Not their 10, and not the middle, 15, without a reason.

65
00:04:07.827 --> 00:04:12.274
If they push back, trade scope: with 12 days, one region thoroughly.

66
00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:14.603
They anchor first?

67
00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.499
Ask where their number comes from, and name yours.

68
00:04:19.542 --> 00:04:23.478
A counteroffer close to an unreasonable number accepts their anchor.

69
00:04:24.475 --> 00:04:28.399
Framing: 95 percent on time, or one in 20 late.

70
00:04:29.441 --> 00:04:30.474
Through loss aversion,

71
00:04:30.672 --> 00:04:34.736
losses weigh more: without this you lose 300 crew hours a month.

72
00:04:35.726 --> 00:04:40.789
Plan your concessions ever smaller: 20, 19, 18.5 days,

73
00:04:40.963 --> 00:04:42.879
and get something back each time.

74
00:04:43.892 --> 00:04:45.146
They know the market better?

75
00:04:45.897 --> 00:04:50.994
Let them open, or name a range with your target at the bottom: 20 to 22.

76
00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:54.613
Always yes makes your yes worth less.

77
00:04:55.318 --> 00:04:58.070
The positive no: yes, no, yes.

78
00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:03.038
Yes to what you protect, a clear no, and then yes to an option.

79
00:05:04.062 --> 00:05:05.792
I'll try sounds like yes.

80
00:05:06.515 --> 00:05:09.220
Give one real reason, then stop talking.

81
00:05:10.259 --> 00:05:12.244
Or offer a trade: what can drop?

82
00:05:12.957 --> 00:05:15.233
Whoever sets the priorities chooses.

83
00:05:16.248 --> 00:05:20.357
A red line is not for trading: safety, integrity, rules.

84
00:05:21.386 --> 00:05:25.751
Prepare your no: no apologising three times, no long reasons.

85
00:05:26.769 --> 00:05:28.070
Thursday, four o'clock:

86
00:05:28.209 --> 00:05:31.494
your boss wants the steering deck reworked by eight tomorrow.

87
00:05:32.462 --> 00:05:36.061
But you promised the planning lead an analysis by Friday noon.

88
00:05:37.072 --> 00:05:39.139
Yes: I want a strong deck too.

89
00:05:39.852 --> 00:05:42.290
No: I can't do both properly tonight.

90
00:05:43.364 --> 00:05:49.343
Yes, with options: three slides and the analysis on time, or the analysis on Monday.
